what is the agency that oversees most agencies

The closest thing the United States has to an agency that “oversees most other agencies” for misconduct, waste, fraud, abuse, and whistleblower protection is the Government Accountability Office (GAO) combined with the network of Inspectors General (IGs) across nearly every federal department and agency.Here’s how it breaks down in practice:Entity
Role
What it actually oversees
Key complaint/reporting portal
Government Accountability Office (GAO)
Congressional watchdog; audits, evaluates, and investigates almost any federal program or agency at Congress’s request
Essentially every executive-branch agency (including DoD, DHS, DOJ, etc.)
FraudNet: https://www.gao.gov/about/what-gao-does/fraudnet
Office of Inspector General (OIG) – there are ~75 of them
Independent oversight unit inside each major department/agency (e.g., DHS OIG, DOJ OIG, HHS OIG, DoD OIG, etc.)
Their own parent department or agency
Most funnel through https://www.oversight.gov (central hub where you pick the agency)
Council of the Inspectors General on Integrity and Efficiency (CIGIE)
Coordinates all the IGs; runs the central reporting site
Acts as the “umbrella” for the entire IG community
https://www.oversight.gov/report-fraud-waste-abuse
Office of Special Counsel (OSC)
Protects federal whistleblowers and investigates prohibited personnel practices across the entire executive branch
All civilian executive-branch employees (not just one agency)
https://osc.gov/Pages/File-Complaint.aspx

In everyday terms:If you want to report fraud, waste, abuse, or mismanagement in almost any federal agency and you’re not sure where to go → start at https://www.oversight.gov (the master IG portal) or https://www.gao.gov/fraudnet.
If you’re a federal employee or contractor blowing the whistle → OSC is usually the safest first stop.

There is no single “super-agency” that directly supervises the day-to-day operations of all others (the President and Congress ultimately do that), but GAO + the IG community (via oversight.gov) is the practical answer to “who watches the watchers” for most federal agencies.

